# UWE Project

UWE (Universeller Welten-Editor) is a self-hosted **Alltags- und Hobby-Betriebssystem**: Daily Admin OS, DnD campaign brain, player portal, and optional local AI — all on the user's own hardware.

## Product domains

| Domain | Location | Purpose |
|--------|----------|---------|
| **Studio** | `apps/studio` | DM app — world editing, admin cockpit, AI workflows |
| **Portal** | `apps/portal` | Player wiki and handouts — server-filtered, no DM leaks |
| **Daily Admin OS** | `apps/studio` (`/today`, `/capture`, …) | Today, Capture, Projects, Workshop, Contracts, Hardware, Life Brain — Studio-only |
| **Core packages** | `packages/*` | Shared data layer, auth, wiki engine, generators |
| **DnD worlds** | `packages/database`, `packages/ai-brain` | Canon, visibility, generator tasks |
| **Life Brain** | `apps/studio/app/life-brain`, `packages/ai-brain` | Personal knowledge — RTX-only retrieval, no Portal |
| **Integrations** | `packages/calendar`, `packages/mail` | Calendar feeds, Mail Center (self-hosted SMTP) |
| **Auth** | `packages/auth`, `packages/security` | Sessions, permissions, route guards |
| **Media** | `packages/assets`, `packages/image-studio` | Uploads, MIME validation, Image Studio jobs |
| **Generators** | `packages/ai-brain` | DnD generator, brain retrieval, privacy guards |
| **Hosting** | Linux + `scripts/uwe-host-*`, `deploy/systemd/uwe.service` | Self-hosted deployment (no Docker/Windows installer as active path) |

## Architecture principles

- Business logic lives in `packages/`, not in Next.js route handlers.
- Studio is write/admin; Portal is read-only player output.
- Never expose `dm_only` content to Portal or static export.
- RTX/local inference stays on the home network; cloud AI gets no world/brain/life context.
- Daily Admin OS data never reaches Portal or static export.
- AI outputs are proposals/drafts — never auto-applied to canon or personal brain.
- Match existing package boundaries (`@uwe/auth`, `@uwe/database`, etc.).

## Agent defaults

- Prefer safe defaults over blocking questions when conventions are clear.
- Keep changes small, reviewable, and scoped to the task.
